# Voice reference terminology

Use these terms in UI copy, comments, commit messages, and agent explanations. Prefer them over ad-hoc wording (“UK library clip”, “character ref”, “EN line”, etc.) unless referring to a concrete code symbol or table name.

## Global voice reference (глобальний референс голосу)

A **third-party** voice from the open Ukrainian reference library (`uk_voice_library` / `character_uk_voices`).

- Linked **per character (voice folder), globally across mods** — not overridden per mod.
- Purpose: improve **Ukrainian pronunciation**. Conditioning only on English game audio tends to produce strong English accent in synthesized Ukrainian.
- In the TTS request it is sent **together with** the local reference when both are enabled (global first, then local).
- Project setting: `voice.uk_library`. Code symbols may still say `ukLibrary` / `useUkLibrary`.

## Local voice reference (локальний референс голосу)

Audio taken from **the game’s own voice files** for that character.

- **Same-line local reference** — original English audio of the line being synthesized (`voice.line_reference` on / mode `line`), when the clip is long enough.
- **Selected-line local reference** — another line of the same character (auto-picked, manually set in the voice editor, or `Sound/Voice/.../_reference.wav`) when the synthesized line is too short, or when same-line mode is off (`voice.line_reference` off / mode `speaker`).
- Purpose: keep the character’s **delivery / timbre / identity** from the game performance.

## Do not confuse

| Term | Is |
|---|---|
| Global reference | Open-library Ukrainian clip (pronunciation) |
| Local reference | In-game character clip (identity / prosody) |

Regen dialog exposes independent **global** and **local** toggles (`global_reference` / `local_reference`). When both are off, synthesis still falls back to this line’s EN audio so Fish Speech receives a speaker clip.

When writing new UI strings, label toggles as **global** / **local** reference, not “library voice” / “character voice”, unless space forces a short label — then keep the tooltip explicit.